#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151885 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90930e15-0827-5e0b-82cf-98263fc756d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "f0520e86-8df9-5090-8d5c-761a4ba20583" :question/math-academy-id "e-6565" :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\n\n- $\\, (2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0$\n\n- $\\,y'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x$\n\n- $\\,y'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x) = 0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n(2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0\n$$\n\nis homogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x\n$$\n\nis inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 3e^x,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0\n$$\n\nin the form $ay'' + by' + cy = f,$ we have\n\n$$\ny'' + x^3 y = 8.\n$$\n\nThis equation is inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 8,$ which is different from zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151886 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "45688683-2788-58bd-8cf5-80feab863551" :question/math-academy-id "e-6566" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $y'' + (y-1)y'+x = 2$\n\n- $y''+y'=x \\cos x$\n\n- $yy'' - y'+ 9 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "A second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny'' + (y-1)y'+x = 2\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $(y-1)y'.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny''+y'=x \\cos x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x) = 1, b(x) = 1, c(x) = 0,$ and $f(x) = x\\cos{x}.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\nyy'' - y'+ 9 = 0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $yy''.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151887 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/id #uuid "1826d315-cef0-5b5f-b654-61733a63bf2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6567" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x^2y'' + xy' = x+2$\n\n- $9y'' + 4y = e^x, \\quad y(0) = 1,  \\quad  y'(4)= 1$\n\n- $y''+xy'+x^2y= \\sin x, \\quad y(1) = 0,  \\quad  y'(1)= 5$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem.**\n\nWith this in mind, let's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- System I is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- System II is not an initial value problem because it consists of a differential equation together with two conditions in *different* locations, $x=0$ and $x=4.$ So this is a **boundary value problem**, not an initial value problem.\n\n- System III is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151888 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ec8603-ae96-5212-88e1-4571d7face6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Associated Homogeneous Equation of a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/id #uuid "4f4f4539-466a-5cc0-9470-8b94d6221093" :question/math-academy-id "e-6568" :question/problem "For the differential equation $2xy'' +y' - y = \\cos x,$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n2xy'' +y' - y = 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152489 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90930e15-0827-5e0b-82cf-98263fc756d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3a1c028-7717-55db-b8a3-fbe577da5ac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149798} {:answer/id #uuid "3558e504-c815-5bdb-904b-6b499c98a79e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3a1c028-7717-55db-b8a3-fbe577da5ac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149798} :answer-field/id #uuid "600fbb74-bdbd-5116-97b9-b2a5cd79d9b0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149797} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5030bc4-4753-5b61-a957-f308fdf7daee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149801} {:answer/id #uuid "de118a19-6e65-5980-a6d7-0def901542e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5030bc4-4753-5b61-a957-f308fdf7daee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149801} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e245b17-4874-510f-a799-adb5a6f3f08b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149800} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df70e34f-2742-563a-b5a9-c191427a8257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149804} {:answer/id #uuid "5edabb40-bdd5-5070-97d3-bcda1993b06e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df70e34f-2742-563a-b5a9-c191427a8257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149804} :answer-field/id #uuid "81c68ad2-f693-5f7e-ba69-d5b504b3c6d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e93ecbe-42b9-5aed-a2ce-1b2b418237f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-337990" :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.$\\,$ The equation $\\,{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}-y=0$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+y={e}^{x}$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y+x=0$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}-y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+y={e}^{x}\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)={e}^{x},$ which is different from zero.\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y+x=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y=-x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=-x,$ which is different from zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90930e15-0827-5e0b-82cf-98263fc756d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0755616e-8d6f-50ce-82cd-8c3124d45f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341388} {:answer/id #uuid "e16c05fe-b3a8-50d5-ab83-3f44716c3fc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e16c05fe-b3a8-50d5-ab83-3f44716c3fc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341389} :answer-field/id #uuid "64dfac85-867c-5d93-abdd-a093526fff34" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341387} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42384a7c-cd2c-5e84-97e8-1be0ef4192ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341391} {:answer/id #uuid "2e727099-ce63-54df-b3c8-6be4852bed67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42384a7c-cd2c-5e84-97e8-1be0ef4192ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341391} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2a7bfec-c698-5162-9d1d-70b6585ae610" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341390} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e407a07-7097-5845-9355-c9ad8823438e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341394} {:answer/id #uuid "c916f550-13d3-5b42-9796-6f02c236fda7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c916f550-13d3-5b42-9796-6f02c236fda7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341395} :answer-field/id #uuid "fee27974-b815-5e20-b40c-bd897c398114" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fc00c12-63c8-559a-aa45-1b909350bd43" :question/math-academy-id "q-337996" :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=7$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){y}^{″}+(1-x){y}^{′}-4y=0$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+xy+{x}^{3}=0$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=7\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=7,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){y}^{″}+(1-x){y}^{′}-4y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+xy+{x}^{3}=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+xy=-{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=-{x}^{3},$ which is different from zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341416 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47bad2b8-8d3e-57d6-82b3-2c3439f672a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341418} {:answer/id #uuid "35ff67c9-ece9-59f1-8612-b8e2b3c84de4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341419} {:answer/id #uuid "6da61333-a591-52aa-9ef7-07f3e1c29e8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341420} {:answer/id #uuid "7558911d-9966-59ef-82bc-a27c1567a02c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341421} {:answer/id #uuid "7aca29ef-7d33-5dd9-8aa3-21f2f8e57080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6da61333-a591-52aa-9ef7-07f3e1c29e8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341420} :answer-field/id #uuid "6626197e-a518-52e5-8075-49de48d89a58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63a0e75a-e8ed-5555-a126-d694412fb15f" :question/math-academy-id "q-338102" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}+y={x}^{2}$\n- $y{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}=x$\n- $5{y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+7{y}^{2}=x$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}+y={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=x,b(x)=0,c(x)=1,$ and $f(x)={x}^{2}.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $y{y}^{″}.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n5{y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+7{y}^{2}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $7{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40c24394-e973-59d1-9167-634cf777f21e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341432} {:answer/id #uuid "8802af8b-4dde-5a58-a51e-084a51ffb842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341433} {:answer/id #uuid "2abac178-ade9-5f83-9fe1-6252892ec145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341434} {:answer/id #uuid "13231aec-e1bb-5d83-b2ea-584488714b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341435} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d74180-5d78-5e19-a40e-3203ce946229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13231aec-e1bb-5d83-b2ea-584488714b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341435} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8a9729b-f977-5b73-a7a7-48f5c43c8f50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec6d918c-1e89-56ba-9337-5b4cab88266e" :question/math-academy-id "q-338533" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(0)=2,\\,y(5)=2$\n- ${y}^{″}+y={x}^{3}$\n- ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(-3)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(-3)=1$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=5$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-3$)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "188e19b8-04d9-5024-a1a0-560681b52a11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341439} {:answer/id #uuid "3557fe35-3d8a-5b9f-8319-034a0b55e292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341440} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ec3ff9-a194-5b32-b7b0-91f6b59a11e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341441} {:answer/id #uuid "5c65fb1c-74b3-5c07-a63e-9a134cc05155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341442} {:answer/id #uuid "7c29f87b-9169-55a0-9fcd-db3d482e3a30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1ec3ff9-a194-5b32-b7b0-91f6b59a11e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341441} :answer-field/id #uuid "75cf2381-7b48-5325-b87b-78fc4e994f54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7b3292e-ae09-5cdf-bbb6-9d5f24a711b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-339256" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+2y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-y=5x$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+4y={x}^{2},\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(4)=2$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$).\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=4$). This is a **boundary value problem.**"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49d4357e-5d94-57d3-bf28-de364df8aa32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341453} {:answer/id #uuid "796af2d8-3cb3-5bf0-9137-370ed0d9102b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341454} {:answer/id #uuid "945961fe-2c6d-529c-87e0-7ab2efe873bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341455} {:answer/id #uuid "7d866803-b1f6-5fe1-bbea-38c8c0b7bbcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341456} {:answer/id #uuid "e65b145a-16e6-5f2a-aaf7-4f776e4acff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49d4357e-5d94-57d3-bf28-de364df8aa32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341453} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d4ced10-83a3-51c8-9db0-13b6654e303b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01fc92bc-36e8-54db-9a2e-368326538d04" :question/math-academy-id "q-93721" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $2{y}^{2}{y}^{″}-{e}^{x}{y}^{′}=x$\n- $\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- ${y}^{″}+17y=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n2{y}^{2}{y}^{″}-{e}^{x}{y}^{′}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $2{y}^{2}{y}^{″}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=\\frac{1}{x},b(x)=1,c(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ and $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+17y=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=1,b(x)=0,c(x)=17,$ and $f(x)=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}]}
